In this study, the effects of various synthesis parameters (such as deposition potentials, deposition times, and concentration of precursors) on the architecture of the \(\mathrm{GOx@Cu-MOF/CF}\) were systematically investigated in order to optimize the immobilization of \(\mathrm{GOx}\) within Cu-MOF layers. At low deposition potential \((1.00\;\mathrm{V})\), the growth of Cu-MOF layer was restricted to the surface of copper foam, forming discrete bottom Cu-MOF layer zones (Fig. 3(a)). With the increase in deposition potential, bottom Cu-MOF layer zones gradually grew up, connecting each other, while some upstanding Cu-MOF sheets grew on the bottom Cu-MOF layer (Figs. 3(b)–3(d)). Accordingly, the quantity of \(\mathrm{GOx}\) encapsulated significantly increased (Fig. S5 in the ESM). Similar morphology evolution trend was observed in the experiments with varying deposition time (Figs. 3(a)–3(b)) or varying concentration of organic ligands
(Fig. S6 in the ESM). In contrast to other synthesis parameters, deposition potential had more significant impact on the formation of \(\mathrm{GOx@Cu-MOF}\) on the copper foam. This is because the deposition potential directly controls the release rate of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) ions, thus affecting the growth rate of MOFs and the encapsulation of enzymes.
Based on above results, natural enzyme \(\mathrm{GOx}\) was successfully immobilized on the 3D porous conducting electrode with the MOF encapsulation strategy. Such an architecture greatly guarantees efficient cascade between enzyme/nanozyme-catalyzed reactions and the sensitive signal acquisition, thereby leading to superior catalytic activity for glucose sensing (Fig. 4(a)). The bio-electrocatalytic detection of glucose over \(\mathrm{GOx@Cu-MOF/CF}\) electrode was conducted in air-saturated \(0.1\mathrm{~M~}\) phosphate buffered saline (PBS) solution at room temperature \((25\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\). Figure 4(b) presents the CV curves of the representative electrode, which clearly shows that the reduction current increased with the successive addition of glucose. In the detection process of glucose, \(\mathrm{GOx}\) embedded within Cu-MOF layer catalyzed glucose to gluconic acid and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), and then Cu-MOF/CF electro-catalyzed the reduction of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) immediately. The measurement results revealed that the electrode exhibited the optimal current response at \(-0.40\;\mathrm{V}\) (Fig. 4(c)). In addition, a well-defined linear relationship between the reduction current and the glucose concentration could be established in the concentration range from 0 to \(6\;\mathrm{mM}\) (Fig. 4(d)). Strikingly, \(\mathrm{GOx@Cu-MOF/CF}\) electrode had much higher sensitivity than the unprotected \(\mathrm{GOx/CF}\) electrode. According to the Lineweaver-Burk plot (Fig. 4(e)), the apparent Michaelis constant \(\left(K_{\mathrm{m}}\right)\) of the \(\mathrm{GOx@MOF/CF}\) electrode was \(0.51\;\mathrm{mM}\), much lower than those previously reported for free \(\mathrm{GOx}\) \((25.7\;\mathrm{mM})\) or MOF encapsulated \(\mathrm{GOx}\) \((\sim39\;\mathrm{mM})\) [39]. The lower \(K_{\mathrm{m}}\) value for the \(\mathrm{GOx@MOF/CF}\) electrode reveals its better affinity for glucose and thus, contributes to its high sensitivity for glucose detection.
The significantly improved glucose sensitivity of \(\mathrm{GOx@MOF/CF}\) electrode could be understood from two aspects. On one hand, high local concentrations of substrates/products are easily generated in such a confined environment within Cu-MOF layer. On the other hand, there are no diffusion barriers for the permeation of the
substrates into Cu-MOF or for the release of the product from Cu-MOF. These two factors greatly increase the cascade efficiency of enzyme catalyzed process and electro-catalyzed process [5, 16]. Of note, it was found that the sensitivity of \(\mathrm{GOx@Cu-MOF}\) modified electrode increased first and then decreased as deposition potentials, times, or concentrations of BTC increased (Figs. 5(a)–5(c)). This is because an excessively thick \(\mathrm{GOx@Cu-MOF}\) layer would affect the diffusion of substrates and products, resulting in the decrease in sensitivity. In addition to the diffusion problem, the aggregation of enzyme caused by high concentration could also inhibit the activity of \(\mathrm{GOx}\) (Fig. 5(d)) [39].
It is well known that enzymes can be easily deactivated by any abrupt changes in their external environment (typically heat). Therefore, stability of immobilized enzyme is an important problem which has to be solved before practical applications. For this purpose, the heat-resistance of \(\mathrm{GOx@MOF/CF}\) was specifically investigated herein. As revealed in Fig. 6(a), the activity of \(\mathrm{GOx@Cu-MOF}\) modified electrode barely decreased \((<2\%)\) when it was incubated in water for \(1\,\mathrm{{h}}\) at \(40\,^{\circ}\!C.\) . Surprisingly, the electrode still retained ca. \(80\%\) of its initial activity after it was incubated at \(80\,^{\circ}\!C.\) . In contrast, the activity of un-encapsulated \(\mathrm{GOx/CF}\) quickly declined with the increase in incubation temperature (Fig. 6(b)). At \(40~^{\circ}\!C,\) the un-encapsulated \(\mathrm{GOx/CF}\) electrode lost over half of its original activity, while its activity at \(80~^{\circ}\!C\) was drastically reduced to below \(10\%\) of the original value.
In general, the thicker the MOF layer, the better its protective effect on the encapsulated \(\mathrm{GO}x,\) which was confirmed by the deposition potential-dependent experiments (Fig. 6(c)). At low deposition potentials, as-formed Cu-MOF layers could not completely cover
the immobilized \(\mathrm{GO}x\) on copper foam due to limited dissolution of \(C\mathbf{u}^{2+}\) ions (Figs. 3(a) and 3(b)). Under these conditions, the exposed \(\mathrm{GO}x\) regions were not protected from high temperature by the Cu-MOF layer. With the increase in deposition potential, the degree of coverage of \(\mathrm{GO}x\) increased significantly, and the thermal stability was improved accordingly. When the Cu-MOF layer was thick enough to encapsulate \(\mathrm{GO}x\) to shield it from high temperature, further increase in Cu-MOF layer thickness did not enhance the thermal stability. Furthermore, the deposition time also had different effects on the thermal stability of \(\mathrm{GO}x@C\mathrm{u-MOF}\) modified electrode (Fig. 6(d)). For the \(\mathrm{GO}x@C\mathrm{u-MOF}\) modified electrodes obtained at long deposition times, thermal stability became worse, which was likely due to the incomplete encapsulation of \(\mathrm{GO}x\) by MOF layer.
Table 1 is the comparison of the performance of the \(\mathrm{GO}x@C\mathrm{u}-\) MOF/CF with that of other previously reported immobilized enzyme sensors [39–45]. For the \(\mathrm{GO}x@C\mathrm{u-MOF/CF},\) there is no diffusion
barrier for substrates to activity sites of enzymes within Cu-MOF nanosheets, compared with bulk MOFs or hydrophobic MOFs [39, 40]. This contributes to the affinity of substrate to enzyme, resulting in lower \(K_{\mathrm{m}}\) of \(\mathrm{GO}x\ @\mathrm{Cu-MOF/CF}\). As for the proposed encapsulation method, the enzymes embedded within the MOF matrices have better thermal stability than those immobilized onto the matrices such as carbon nanotubes due to the protection of MOF shell [41–45]. In a word, the proposed \(\mathrm{GO}x\ @\mathrm{Cu-MOF/CF}\) sensor exhibited better affinity to glucose and thermal stability benefitting from the preparation method of Cu-MOF nanosheets grown around \(\mathrm{GO}x\) via the biomimetic mineralization.
The stability of \(\mathrm{GO}x\ @\mathrm{Cu-MOF}\) modified electrode was also studied by using SEM. The surface of the copper foam electrode was still covered completely by a layer of upstanding nanosheets after incubated in water at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{~h~}\) and subsequent test reaction (Fig. 7(a)). From the high magnification SEM images of the \(\mathrm{GO}x\ @\mathrm{Cu-MOF}\) (Figs. 7(b) and 7(c)), there was no damage found on the surface of nanosheets. These data further confirmed the excellent thermal stability of \(\mathrm{GO}x\ @\mathrm{Cu-MOF}\) modified electrode. This MOF encapsulated enzyme structure can be potentially applied for efficient photo-thermal therapy and other platforms subjected to harsh operating conditions.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, this work demonstrated the one-step synergetic fabrication of \(\mathrm{GO}x\ @\mathrm{Cu-MOF}\) modified copper foam multienzyme system through electrochemical oxidation and biomimetic mineralization, and its use as a glucose sensing electrode. This construction process is under moderate conditions (room temperature), significantly reducing damage to enzymes. The open porous structure of copper foam electrode allows the substrates access to the sensing sites, while the MOF layer provides good protection for \(\mathrm{GO}x\) embedded inside. The as-prepared electrode exhibited significantly enhanced sensitivity and thermal stability in glucose sensing because the electrode design facilitated the cascade catalytic process between \(\mathrm{GO}x\)-catalyzed glucose oxidation and electro-catalyzed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) reduction. Therefore, this work offers a simple synthetic route to immobilize specific enzymes. Considering its high stability, this MOF encapsulated enzyme structure can be potentially applied for efficient photo-thermal therapy and other platforms subjected to harsh operating conditions.
